# AegisNet lab rules (Milestone 2, Chunk 13; ADR-021).
#
# Alert-only by construction. Every rule below starts with the `alert` action; `drop`,
# `reject` and `rejectsrc` are IPS actions and a test asserts that no rule in this file
# uses one, so the lab cannot be turned into an inline blocker by editing rules alone.
#
# The sid range 9100000-9199999 is reserved for this lab, which keeps lab-generated
# alerts distinguishable from the synthetic corpus (9000001-9000002) and from any real
# ruleset an operator might add later. No rule here is downloaded; the lab network has no
# route out and `suricata-update` never runs.

# A benign marker the lab's own HTTP client sets on every well-behaved request, so the
# operator can see an `alert` record with a known cause next to the flow that caused it.
alert http any any -> any any (msg:"AEGISNET-LAB benign marker header"; flow:established,to_server; http.header_names; content:"X-Aegisnet-Lab"; nocase; classtype:not-suspicious; sid:9100001; rev:1;)

# The credential-failure shape D-002 reads: the lab target answers 401 to a request that omits the operator marker header — no credential
# is sent and none exists; the generator's
# deliberately wrong Basic credential, and this rule turns each one into an alert.
alert http any any -> any any (msg:"AEGISNET-LAB authentication failure"; flow:established,to_client; http.stat_code; content:"401"; classtype:attempted-recon; sid:9100002; rev:1;)

# The horizontal sweep D-001 reads: many SYNs from one source in a short window. The
# threshold keeps one alert per source per minute; the flow records carry the detail.
alert tcp any any -> any any (msg:"AEGISNET-LAB connection sweep"; flags:S,12; threshold:type threshold, track by_src, count 20, seconds 60; classtype:attempted-recon; sid:9100003; rev:1;)
